body:
Looks like this is due to this:
https://github.com/diesel-rs/diesel/blob/7ff5e6327d5e88095cb4b342da13bf06d6d0d1cd/diesel/src/pg/expression/array_comparison.rs#L75-L81
which seems not really correct (looks like `Any<Expr>` is not really an expression by itself, and the current writing would allow it to also appear in a select).

It looks like one way to fix it would be to introduce something like:
```rust
trait AsExpressionOrArrayComparison<T> {
    type ExpressionOrArrayCmp: ExpressionOrArrayComparison<SqlType=T>;
    fn as_expression() -> Self::ExpressionOrArrayCmp;
}
impl<T, ST> AsExpressionOrArrayComparison<ST> for T: AsExpression<ST> { ... }
impl<Expr, ST> ExpressionOrArrayComparison for Any<Expr>
where
    Expr: Expression<SqlType = Array<ST>>,
    ST: SqlType + TypedExpressionType,
{
    type SqlType = ST;
}
```
and then use that instead of directly `AsExpression/Expression` in https://github.com/diesel-rs/diesel/blob/fed0da6a46baa2703872bc8bc788891913495ae1/diesel/src/expression_methods/global_expression_methods.rs#L24-L30

That would probably require updating https://github.com/diesel-rs/diesel/blob/0ec6a0ce762922eaa7264dcbd3b4707c4d1aebee/diesel/src/expression/helper_types.rs#L16 (or introduce a new similar one for `AsExpressionOrArrayComparison`).

I'm not sure to what extent we want to make the same changes for
```rust
impl<Expr, ST> Expression for All<Expr>
where
    Expr: Expression<SqlType = Array<ST>>,
    ST: SqlType + TypedExpressionType,
{
    type SqlType = ST;
}
```
as this seems to behave a bit differently: select works.
https://github.com/diesel-rs/diesel/blob/7ff5e6327d5e88095cb4b342da13bf06d6d0d1cd/diesel/src/pg/expression/array_comparison.rs#L129

@hi-rustin Every part in diesels query dsl ast currently implements `Expression`, which contains information about the type of the actual expression. The `Eq` operator is now simply a that takes two expressions of the same type as argument while the operator itself is an `Expression` of the type `Bool`. I think [this example](https://github.com/diesel-rs/diesel/blob/c0b613024adf970dc888453c984d42e86bad6b3e/diesel/src/expression/operators.rs#L189-L211) shows the basic idea quite good. 

On the other hand `Any` is currently also implemented as normal `Expression` that converts an `Expression` of the type  `Array<ST>` to the inner type `ST`. (See [here](https://github.com/diesel-rs/diesel/blob/c0b613024adf970dc888453c984d42e86bad6b3e/diesel/src/pg/expression/array_comparison.rs#L75-L81)). This does not really match the implementation in postgresql, as `any` is really some sort of custom thing that is only allowed in certain places.

We can fix that by doing the following steps:
1. Remove the the `Expression` impl for `any` (that one linked above).
2. We need to make sure that `something = ANY(…)` is valid after the first change. Now this does not work with the simplified `Eq` version presented above. We need to teach `Eq` to accept any `Expression` or the `any` operator as right site of the expression. For this we need to change the trait bounds [here](https://github.com/diesel-rs/diesel/blob/c0b613024adf970dc888453c984d42e86bad6b3e/diesel/src/expression_methods/global_expression_methods.rs#L28). A simple way to model the `Expression` or `any` condition as trait bound is to introduce two new traits:

```rust
trait AsExpressionOrArrayComparison<T> {
    type ExpressionOrArrayCmp: ExpressionOrArrayComparison<SqlType = T>;

    fn as_expression(self) -> Self::ExpressionOrArrayCmp;
}

trait ExpressionOrArrayComparison {
    type SqlType;
}
```

This mirrors the existing `AsExpression` and `Expression` trait. (`AsExpression` describes how to transform rust values into query ast nodes. It is implemented for all types implementing `Expression`  (as they are already query ast nodes) and for all types implementing `ToSql` (those generate bind parameters in the query ast)).

Now we provide 2 implementations for this trait:
* One for any type `T` that already implements `AsExpression<T>`:
```rust
impl<T, ST> AsExpressionOrArrayComparison<ST> for T 
where T: AsExpression<ST> 
{ 
    type ExpressionOrArrayCmp = T::Expression;

    fn as_expression(self) -> T::Expression {
        <T as AsExpression<ST>>::as_expression(self)
    }
 }

impl<T> ExpressionOrArrayComparison for T 
where T: Expression
{
    type SqlType = T::SqlType;
}
```
* One for the `any` type:
```rust
impl<Expr, ST> ExpressionOrArrayComparison for Any<Expr>
where
    Expr: Expression<SqlType = Array<ST>>,
    ST: SqlType + TypedExpressionType,
{
    type SqlType = ST
    
}

impl<Expr, ST> AsExpressionOrArrayComparison<ST> for Any<Expr>
where Self: ExpressionOrArrayComparison<SqlType = ST>
{
    type ExpressionOrArrayCmp = Self;

    fn as_expression(self) -> Self {
        self
    }
}
```

3. Now it we just need to change the definition of `ExpressionMethods::eq` [here](https://github.com/diesel-rs/diesel/blob/c0b613024adf970dc888453c984d42e86bad6b3e/diesel/src/expression_methods/global_expression_methods.rs#L28) to use the new `AsExpressionOrArrayComparison` instead of the existing `AsExpression` trait. This implies that also the [return type](https://github.com/diesel-rs/diesel/blob/c0b613024adf970dc888453c984d42e86bad6b3e/diesel/src/expression/helper_types.rs#L20) needs to be changed (Replace the `AsExpr` part there with the corresponding new version). Additional note: This also applies to a bunch of other operators. We should check the postgres manual here.
